## ***********************************************************************
## **
## **  GPB.INC.BL -- the GP.BASIC keyword definitions for BASLOAD.
## **
## **  #INCLUDE "GPB.INC.BL" at the top of any BASL source that uses GP
## **  keywords. BASLOAD only knows the ROM's keywords; this teaches it
## **  the GP tokens so GP.DO parses instead of raising a syntax error.
## **  The host-side tokeniser (bin/tokenise.zip) does not need this file
## **  -- it learns the same tokens from c64tokens.py at build time.
## **
## **  THE TOKEN VALUES ARE THE ABI. They mirror getGP() in
## **  source/common-scripts/c64tokens.py exactly: $CE7F down, one per
## **  keyword, allocated DOWNWARD and never renumbered. When a keyword
## **  is added there, add it here in the same change.
## **
## **  A PRG containing these tokens is compile-only: the ROM cannot
## **  LIST or RUN it, because nothing in BASIC is behind $CE7x.
## **
## ***********************************************************************
#IFNDEF GP.DEFS
#DEFINE GP.DEFS 1

## Loops -- GP.DO <count> ... GP.LOOP, count 0 or omitted = forever.
## GP.EXITDO leaves the innermost GP.DO early, closing its frame, so it
## is the leak-free exit; jumping out with GOTO abandons the frame.

#TOKEN GP.DO 52863
#TOKEN GP.LOOP 52862
#TOKEN GP.EXITDO 52861

## Machine code. GP.CALL sets the registers from its arguments (all optional,
## all defaulting to 0) and the four value words read the results back. They
## share SYS's $030C-$030F, so they also read what a plain SYS left behind.
##
## WARNING: put the machine code in BANKED RAM ($A000-$BFFF), NOT $0400-$07FF.
## Stock X16 BASIC leaves $0400 free for the user; a COMPILED GPC program does
## not -- that page holds runtime state (stringHighMemory, storeStartHigh,
## variableStartPage ...) and POKEing code over it corrupts the program silently.

#TOKEN GP.CALL 52860
#TOKEN GP.A 52859
#TOKEN GP.X 52858
#TOKEN GP.Y 52857
#TOKEN GP.C 52856

## Strings. GP.INSTR is the search GPC otherwise has no form of at all (no
## INSTR, nothing). GP.STRPTR returns the address of [ActLen][Data] -- length
## AT that address, first character at +1, and the block's capacity at -2. With
## GP.CALL that lets machine code fill a BASIC string in place and set its
## length, which stock BASIC cannot do at all.
##
## Splitting that address for GP.CALL, do NOT use "P AND 255": AND is 16-bit
## SIGNED and the string heap lives above 32767, so it raises OUT OF RANGE.
## Use GP.LOBYTE(P) and GP.HIBYTE(P) -- see their entry at the end of this
## file. (The old advice, H = INT(P/256) : L = P - H*256, is still correct,
## just longer and easier to get wrong.)

#TOKEN GP.INSTR 52855
#TOKEN GP.STRPTR 52854


## In-place string statements. Each takes a STRING VARIABLE (never a literal
## or an expression -- the compiler rejects those) and modifies it where it
## sits. GP.TRIM strips spaces from both ends, GP.LTRIM from the left only,
## GP.RTRIM from the right only. GP.UPPER / GP.LOWER convert case, A-Z only,
## leaving digits, punctuation and PETSCII graphics untouched.
##
## There is NO GP.PAD: padding GROWS a string, and these commands cannot grow
## one past the capacity it was born with. Use STRHELP.PAD from
## "STRHELP.INC.BL" instead -- plain BASIC assignment, which reallocates.

#TOKEN GP.TRIM 52853
#TOKEN GP.LTRIM 52852
#TOKEN GP.UPPER 52851
#TOKEN GP.LOWER 52850
#TOKEN GP.RTRIM 52849

## GP.COMP(a$,b$) compares WITHOUT case: -1 if a$ sorts before b$, 0 if they
## match, 1 if after. Length breaks a tie, so "abc" sorts before "ABCD".
## Use it for a case-blind equality test -- IF GP.COMP(A$,B$) = 0 -- which
## plain "=" cannot do, and as the comparator when ordering names.

#TOKEN GP.COMP 52848

## GP.SORT a$() [,descending] [,foldcase] -- shell sort the array in place.
## The EMPTY PARENTHESES ARE REQUIRED: in BASIC A$ and A$() are different
## variables, so without them the compiler finds the scalar and sorts
## nothing. Both options default to 0 (ascending, case-sensitive) and any
## non-zero value turns them on. Maximum 254 as the DIM bound (255 items).
## Never-assigned elements count as empty strings and sort to the front.

#TOKEN GP.SORT 52847

## GP.ARRPTR(a()) -- the address of element ZERO of an array, so machine
## code called with GP.CALL can work on it in bulk: fill, checksum, min/max,
## sprite batching. The header is already skipped. STRIDE IS YOURS to add:
## 2 bytes per element for a string array (each element is a POINTER to the
## string's block, so follow it and see GP.STRPTR for the layout), 6 for a
## numeric one. Multi-dimensional arrays are rejected.
##
## Empty parentheses required; GP.ARRPTR(A(3)) is a syntax error, add 3*2
## or 3*6 yourself. Same splitting rule as GP.STRPTR -- the address is
## usually above 32767, so GP.LOBYTE / GP.HIBYTE, never P AND 255.

#TOKEN GP.ARRPTR 52846

## Screen stash. GP.STASH bank,x,y,w,h copies a text rectangle into a
## banked RAM bank; GP.RESTR bank puts it back where it came from, and
## GP.RESTR bank,x,y pastes it somewhere else. The stash carries a 4-byte
## header (w,h,x,y) so the restore needs only the bank.
##
## A BANK IS 8K and a cell is 2 bytes, so at most 4094 cells fit -- a full
## 80x60 screen is 9,600 bytes and will NOT fit. Stash panels, not screens.
## The limit is checked per row, before the write that would cross out of
## the bank, so too large is an error and never a corrupted next bank.

#TOKEN GP.STASH 52845
#TOKEN GP.RESTR 52844

## Text drawing, written STRAIGHT INTO VERA -- no KERNAL, which is why it is
## several times faster than PRINT (GPC's character output makes two KERNAL
## calls per character, and BSOUT carries scroll, quote mode and cursor work).
##
##   GP.BOX x,y,w,h [,style] [,col]     frame only, styles 0-5:
##                                      0 solid  1 dither  2 single line
##                                      3 rounded  4 thick  5 thick shaded
##   GP.FILL x,y,w,h,char [,col]        char is PETSCII, e.g. ASC(" ")
##   GP.PRINTAT x,y,text$ [,col]
##
## THE COLOUR IS OPTIONAL AND DEFAULTS TO WHAT "COLOR" LAST SET -- the same
## colour a PRINT would have used. Supplied, it is one byte packed exactly as
## the X16 packs it: background * 16 + foreground. 255 is a REAL colour here
## (light grey on light grey), not a "leave it alone" marker.
##
## The two optionals on GP.BOX cannot be skipped over: write the style if you
## want the colour. GP.BOX X,Y,W,H,,7 is a syntax error, GP.BOX X,Y,W,H,0,7
## is what you meant.
##
## WARNING -- THESE DO NOT MOVE THE CURSOR PRINT USES. Nothing here calls the
## KERNAL, so a plain PRINT after a GP.PRINTAT carries on from wherever the
## KERNAL still thinks the cursor is, NOT after the text just drawn. Use
## LOCATE before PRINT, or stay in one world or the other.
##
## Nothing is clipped. Off the right edge wraps to the next row; off the
## bottom writes past the end of the screen map. Zero width or height draws
## nothing, which is the one case a computed size reaches by accident.

#TOKEN GP.BOX 52843
#TOKEN GP.FILL 52842
#TOKEN GP.PRINTAT 52841

## GP.MENU AND GP.SEL ARE GONE, replaced by "MENUHELP.INC.BL" -- a menu
## written in ordinary GPB commands. It was 462 bytes of assembly plus 11
## of storage, carried by every program that used any GPB keyword at all,
## and adding gamepad support to it was what made the cost stop being
## worth it. The BASL module does the same job -- cursor keys, RETURN, ESC,
## case-blind hotkeys that choose their row, must-select / keep-mark /
## no-wrap, and the SNES pad -- for no runtime bytes, and only the programs
## that want a menu pay for it.
##
##      #INCLUDE "MENUHELP.INC.BL"
##      MENUHELP.X = 8 : MENUHELP.Y = 6
##      MENUHELP.WIDTH = 24 : MENUHELP.COUNT = 4
##      MENUHELP.ITEM$(1) = " NEW GAME"        ... and so on
##      MENUHELP.ATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.TEXT)
##      MENUHELP.HIATTR = THEME.CLR(THEME.HILITE)
##      MENUHELP.HOT$ = "NLOQ"
##      GOSUB MENUHELP.RUN                     answer in MENUHELP.SEL
##
## The one behaviour that did NOT survive is the colourless highlight. The
## assembly swapped the cell's attribute nibbles, so it needed no colours
## at all; the module prints the row again, so it wants one -- and
## MENUHELP.HIATTR = 0 asks for that same inversion back.

##
## GP.SELECT <expr>            evaluate once, then test it against each alternative
## GP.CASE <expr> [,<expr>..]  run the body below if any of them matches
## GP.OTHER                    run the body below if nothing above matched
## GP.ENDSEL                   close the select -- REQUIRED, it frees the selector
##
##   GP.SELECT K
##   GP.CASE 13
##     PRINT "RETURN"
##   GP.CASE 17, 145
##     PRINT "CURSOR"
##   GP.OTHER
##     PRINT "SOMETHING ELSE"
##   GP.ENDSEL
##
## This is NOT a replacement for ON x GOTO/GOSUB, which is a real skip
## table and still the right answer for a dense 1..n index. It is for
## the SPARSE selector -- key codes, state machines - where ON cannot go.
##
## Case values are ordinary numeric expressions, not just constants, and
## are compared in the order written; the first match wins and nothing
## below it runs. Nothing matching with no GP.OTHER is not an error, the
## whole select is simply skipped. Selects may be nested.
##
## Do not GOTO out of a select: GP.ENDSEL is what releases the selector,
## and jumping past it leaves the frame open. GP.EXITDO out of a loop
## that contains one is fine -- that cleans up on its way.

#TOKEN GP.SELECT 52838
#TOKEN GP.CASE 52837
#TOKEN GP.OTHER 52836
#TOKEN GP.ENDSEL 52835

##
## GP.IF <expr> THEN         run the body below if the condition is true
## GP.ELSEIF <expr> THEN     ... otherwise try this one
## GP.ELSE                   ... otherwise run this one
## GP.ENDIF                  close the IF -- REQUIRED
##
##   GP.IF N < 0 THEN
##     PRINT "NEGATIVE"
##   GP.ELSEIF N = 0 THEN
##     PRINT "ZERO"
##   GP.ELSE
##     PRINT "POSITIVE"
##   GP.ENDIF
##
## A BLOCK if, and the block form is the only form: each of the four
## keywords must be ALONE ON ITS LINE. THEN is required and nothing may
## follow it -- GP.IF X > 5 THEN PRINT is a syntax error, not a one line
## IF. Stock IF ... THEN is untouched and still there for that.
##
## GP.ELSEIF may repeat as often as you like and GP.ELSE is optional;
## nothing matching with no GP.ELSE is not an error, the whole IF is
## simply skipped. IFs may be nested, and may be nested inside a
## GP.SELECT case body or a GP.DO loop.
##
## Unlike GP.SELECT there is NO frame to release, so a GOTO out of a
## GP.IF is safe -- GP.ENDIF exists only to mark where the block ends.
## The cost is 12 bytes of runtime for the whole construct, because
## every one of its four opcodes reuses a handler that was already
## there. Conditions are numeric, as everywhere else.

#TOKEN GP.IF 52830
#TOKEN GP.ELSEIF 52829
#TOKEN GP.ELSE 52828
#TOKEN GP.ENDIF 52827

## GP.ASM / GP.ENDASM -- inline 65C02 assembly, assembled at compile time.
##
##   #REM 1
##   GP.ASM
##   REM lda #72
##   REM jsr $ffd2
##   REM ldx #5
##   REM loop: dex
##   REM bne loop
##   GP.ENDASM
##   #REM 0
##
## The body rides in REM statements, one instruction per line, because
## BASLOAD stores REM text BYTE FOR BYTE -- ORA, AND, EOR and ROR all
## survive there, and outside a REM they are tokenised into keywords and
## destroyed. Lower case is fine; it is upshifted for you.
##
## #REM 1 IS REQUIRED, and #REM 0 is BASLOAD's default. With REM output
## off the body is stripped before the compiler ever sees it, so the two
## delimiters are real keywords rather than REMs: an empty GP.ASM block
## is then a named error (BLOCK MISMATCH) instead of a program that
## silently contains no code at all. Turn REMs back off after GP.ENDASM.
##
## COSTS NOTHING AT RUN TIME. A block is five bytes of p-code plus the
## instructions themselves, and it reuses handlers that are already in
## every program -- so a program whose only GP.BASIC keyword is GP.ASM
## does NOT pull in the 2K GP runtime block.
##
## LABELS.  name: on its own line or in front of an instruction. Six
## characters are significant. Labels belong to their block: the same
## name in two blocks is two different labels. BNE/BEQ/BRA and the rest
## take one, and a branch further than 127 bytes is OUT OF RANGE at
## COMPILE time, on the line it is on. JMP and JSR take one too.
##
## {VAR} is the ADDRESS OF A BASIC VARIABLE'S SLOT -- so LDA {N%} reads
## it and STA {N%} writes it, in the slot BASIC itself uses:
##
##   {N}  {N%}  {N$}   the scalar's slot
##   {N()}             the ARRAY's slot, which holds the base address of
##                     its data -- an element is two steps, as it is
##                     through GP.ARRPTR
##
## >> {VAR} DOES NOT WORK THROUGH BASLOAD. BASLOAD renames variables --
## >> N% becomes A% -- and does not rename REM text with them, so the
## >> name in the braces is no longer the name BASIC uses. GPC refuses a
## >> {VAR} it cannot find rather than creating one nothing reads, so you
## >> get an error and not a wrong answer. It works when the source is
## >> tokenised by the host tokeniser instead. See ASM.EXP.BL.
##
## NO EXPRESSIONS: {N%}+1 and LABEL+2 are not understood. Use an index
## register. Zero page or absolute is chosen by the OPERAND -- $34 is
## zero page, $0034 is absolute -- and every 65C02 mode is available
## except BBR/BBS/RMB/SMB.
##
## Bodies may also come from a file, which is the same thing spelt
## differently -- #INCLUDE splices it in verbatim:
##
##   #REM 1
##   GP.ASM
##   #INCLUDE "MACPTR.ASM"
##   GP.ENDASM
##   #REM 0
#TOKEN GP.ASM 52826
#TOKEN GP.ENDASM 52825

## GP.CONTAINS(a$,b$) is -1 if b$ occurs anywhere in a$, 0 if it does not.
## CASE SENSITIVE -- it compares raw bytes. It is the readable spelling of the
## yes/no question; use GP.INSTR when you want the POSITION instead of a flag,
## and GP.COMP for a case-blind test.
##
## An EMPTY needle is 0, not -1: GP.INSTR reports "not found" for a zero length
## needle, and this is built on it.
##
## COMPOSITE -- it has no runtime code of its own. The compiler expands it into
## GP.INSTR(a$,b$)<>0 and the object is byte for byte identical to writing that
## out by hand. So it costs nothing to use, and nothing to have.

#TOKEN GP.CONTAINS 52834

## GP.HIBYTE(n) / GP.LOBYTE(n) split an address into the two bytes a 6502
## actually takes: HIBYTE is INT(n/256), the 256 byte page, and LOBYTE is
## MOD(n,256), the offset within it. Everything that eats an address one byte
## at a time wants them -- GP.CALL's registers, VERA's $9F20/$9F21 -- and
## GP.STRPTR / GP.ARRPTR exist to hand an address over in the first place.
##
##   P = GP.STRPTR(A$)
##   GP.CALL $A000, GP.LOBYTE(P), GP.HIBYTE(P)
##
## USE THESE RATHER THAN THE LONG FORM ABOVE. "N AND 255" is the spelling that
## looks right and is a live bug -- AND is 16 bit SIGNED and these addresses
## are above 32767, so it raises OUT OF RANGE instead of masking. GP.LOBYTE is
## built on MOD, which is not on that path.
##
## Range 0 to 65535. COMPOSITE, like GP.CONTAINS -- no runtime code at all.

#TOKEN GP.HIBYTE 52833
#TOKEN GP.LOBYTE 52832

## GP.ISEMPTY(a$) is -1 when a$ has zero length, 0 otherwise. A string of
## SPACES is not empty -- GP.TRIM it first if that is what you meant.
##
## It is for READABILITY and nothing else. It compiles to the same four bytes
## as LEN(A$)=0, and IF A$="" costs about the same again, so do not choose
## between the three on size. COMPOSITE -- no runtime code.

#TOKEN GP.ISEMPTY 52831

#ENDIF
